The hard deny list now matches commands instead of substrings. It's committed as fdf2aa30. Build, all 5925 tests, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check all pass.

I hit the bug myself during this session. This session still runs the old binary, so its bash refused my own grep and my commit message. I had to write the message to a file and commit with git commit -F.

What changed
• **New src/hard_deny.rs:** `hard_deny_match` splits the command into shell words the way a shell would. A quoted string counts as one argument, never as a command, so `echo "rm -rf /"`, grep 'rm -rf /' src/ and commit messages that mention it all pass.
• **Rules:**
  • **Deleting root or home:** any recursive flag spelling (`-fr`, `-r -f`, `-Rf`, `--recursive`) with a target of `/`, `/*`, `~` or `$HOME`. `--no-preserve-root` alone is also refused.
  • **`mkfs:** only when it's the command being run, or has a /dev/ argument. man mkfs` passes.
  • **`dd:** only when it writes to a real device. of=/dev/null, /dev/stdout and /dev/stderr` are allowed, so writing an image file passes.
  • **Fork bomb:** still a substring match, but with spaces removed, so the usual spaced form :(){ :|:& };: is now caught as well.
  • **Nested shells:** bash -c and eval are checked up to 3 levels deep. Past that it falls back to a substring scan that refuses rather than allows.
• **`src/tools.rs:** re-exports hard_deny_refusal and HARD_DENY_PATTERNS` from the new module. Both the parent and child bash still go through that one function. The file shrank from 5068 to 5053 lines, and I updated its entry in the module-size register.
• **HARD_DENY_PATTERNS** keeps its five strings, which now act as the names of the rules. That keeps the refusal text byte-identical.
• **Wording quirk I kept on purpose:** a refusal of `rm -fr /` still says it "contains 'rm -rf /'". The quoted text is the rule's name, not a literal match. I followed the task's instruction not to change the message beyond the label.

Tests (all in `src/hard_deny.rs`)
• 41 commands that must be refused, each checked against its exact rule name, and 23 near misses that must pass.
• Plus checks that each list entry's basic form is refused, that the refusal text is unchanged, that a disabled rule is skipped, and that nesting past the depth limit is still refused.
• **Positive control:** I made `hard_deny_match` always return `None`, ran the tests and restored it in one command. 5 tests failed by name (e.g. `should refuse: "rm -rf /"), the near-miss test stayed green as it should, and after restoring all 6 passed. No NEUTERED` marker is left in the file.

Still not handled (written in the module doc; all of these refuse rather than allow):
• A heredoc line that reads like a root delete is treated as a command and refused.
• `$(…)`, backticks and lines with an unclosed quote aren't parsed. They get a second check with the quotes removed instead.
• `rm -rf /usr, find / -delete and > /dev/sda` were never on the list. This is a guard against accidents, not a sandbox.

I also added an ARCHITECTURE.md entry. The commit mentions #977. The user docs at docs/src/configuration/permissions.md:358 were still accurate, so I left them alone.
